The democratization of knowledge plays a key aspect in the era and the evolution of printers 
in the early 1920s, as the major developments occurred during this period, and the technology behind 
printers exceeded the expectations with various mechanical improvements. In 1938 Charles Hull invented 
the first ever xerography, also known as a photocopy on October 22, 1938, when the first xerography image was made. 
Before mass printing, information was tightly guarded, books were hand copied by scribes, making literature an expensive, 
rare luxury reserved exclusively for the wealth ruling class also they were well known as the nobles of the land in the early days and 
the religious elites in the area as they got the first use case of the early printers. For instance, in the early days the use of mechanical press 
shattered this monopoly, by making books cheap and reproducible at scale, revolutionary ideas, scientific texts, and ancient philosophy were directly 
and indirectly placed into the hands of the public.  

As many people explored by media theorist Marshall Mc Luhan, the widespread availability of printed text caused psychological and cultural schism. 
Society shifted from an auditory, oral culture. It is a place where information was passed by speech and memory. This collective cognitive shift altered how humans processed logic, 
standardized languages across regions, and ultimately enabled the structural rise of modern nation-states. 
The speed of the printing press directly enabled historical movements that eventually established powers and 
knowledge across the lands. A prime example is the protestant Reformation, as martin Luther famously called printing "the ultimate gift of God", 
because it allowed over 300,000 copies of his 95 Theses to be distributed across Europe in just three years. Without the rapid replication of the press. 
Such localized dissent would have likely been suppressed by church gatekeepers. Before the mid 19th century, printing presses were flatbed machines that required operators t feed individual sheets of paper manually. 
In early 1843, Richard March Hoe invented the rotary printing press paper manually, which placed the type on a revolving cylinder to print continuously against a second cylinder. 
This mechanical leap was amplified by the introduction of web fed printing, which used massive, continuous rolls of paper instead of individual sheets. This combination revolutionized industrial media, 
allowing newspaper plants to scale up from printing a few hundred pages an hour to pump out tens of thousands of copies per hour, driving the rise of the mass-media era with the spread of infinite knowledge and 
wisdom across all the lands in the early 1840s and 1860s, and it was available for all people including the nobles, religious and the poor in the early days. 
